An **applet** was a Java program that ran inside a web browser. An **application** is a stand-alone program with a `main` method.

*Dated note:* the applet API was deprecated in Java 9 and marked for removal in Java 17, and applets no longer run in modern browsers (as of 2024). The syllabus still names them.

**Compilation and execution:** source code (`.java`) is compiled by `javac` into **bytecode** (`.class`). The **Java Virtual Machine (JVM)** interprets the bytecode, or compiles it just in time to machine (object) code. Because the same bytecode runs on any computer that has a JVM, Java is **platform independent** ("write once, run anywhere").

```java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Welcome to Java");
    }
}
```

Output: `Welcome to Java`

An **object** has **state** (data members) and **behaviour** (methods). A **class** is a blueprint (template) for objects and a user-defined data type; it is an **object factory**, because many objects can be created from one class with `new`. Each object is an **instance** of its class and has its own copy of the data members.

```java
public class Ticket {
    int seats;
    int booked;

    void book(int n) {
        if (booked + n <= seats)
            booked = booked + n;
    }

    void show() {
        System.out.println(booked + " of " + seats + " seats booked");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Ticket x = new Ticket();
        Ticket y = new Ticket();
        x.seats = 4;
        y.seats = 2;
        x.book(3);
        y.book(3);
        x.show();
        y.show();
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
3 of 4 seats booked
0 of 2 seats booked
```

Data members receive default values (0, 0.0, false, null) when an object is created. A variable of class type holds a **reference**; assigning one such variable to another copies the reference, so both names refer to one object.

**Message passing:** objects communicate by calling each other's methods. The call is the message.

```java
class Battery {
    int percent = 90;

    void use(int n) {
        percent = percent - n;
    }
}

class Phone {
