```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Fan {
    int speed;              // present speed, 0 means off
    int max = 5;            // highest speed the fan has

    void setSpeed(int s) {
        if (s >= 0 && s <= max)
            speed = s;
    }

    void faster() {
        if (speed < max)
            speed++;
    }

    void slower() {
        if (speed > 0)
            speed--;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Speed now: " + speed);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int start = sc.nextInt();
        int up = sc.nextInt();
        int down = sc.nextInt();
        Fan f = new Fan();
        f.setSpeed(start);
        f.display();
        for (int i = 1; i <= up; i++)
            f.faster();
        f.display();
        for (int i = 1; i <= down; i++)
            f.slower();
        f.display();
    }
}
```

**Sample input** (starting speed, faster presses, slower presses): `1 6 2`

**Output**

```text
Speed now: 1
Speed now: 5
Speed now: 3
```

**Variable description**

| Variable | Type | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| speed | int | present speed of the fan (state) |
| max | int | highest speed allowed |
| s | int | parameter of setSpeed(): the speed to set |
| start, up, down | int | starting speed, number of faster presses, number of slower presses |
| i | int | loop counter |
| f | Fan | the object created in main |

The state of the object, speed, is changed only through its methods, and the checks inside the methods keep it between 0 and max.

